1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:05,933
Last week, out of nowhere, my eight-year-old asked me this.

2
00:00:06,033 --> 00:00:10,300
"Dad, what's the difference between a hotel, a motel and a youth hostel?"

3
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:14,000
And this is how I answered.

4
00:00:14,100 --> 00:00:15,567
"Uh... well..."

5
00:00:16,267 --> 00:00:18,333
"Um, the prices are a bit different."

6
00:00:19,033 --> 00:00:23,100
I dodged the question by talking about price,

7
00:00:23,200 --> 00:00:28,267
and looking back, I never gave the real reason at all.

8
00:00:28,967 --> 00:00:33,800
We lump all three together as just "a place to sleep."

9
00:00:33,900 --> 00:00:38,333
But they were born for completely different reasons.

10
00:00:38,433 --> 00:00:45,500
So today, let's trace who each of these three words was made for.

11
00:00:46,200 --> 00:00:50,533
The word "hotel" comes from the French "hôtel."

12
00:00:50,633 --> 00:00:54,800
It originally meant a nobleman's townhouse.

13
00:00:54,900 --> 00:01:01,967
Keeping guest rooms there for traveling nobles is how hotels began.

14
00:01:02,667 --> 00:01:09,733
The motel is different. In 1920s America, cars suddenly became common.

15
00:01:09,833 --> 00:01:16,433
On a long drive, you needed somewhere to park at night and sleep right beside your car.

16
00:01:16,533 --> 00:01:21,967
So "motor" and "hotel" were combined into a new word: "motel."

17
00:01:22,067 --> 00:01:25,033
It was lodging built for people traveling by car.

18
00:01:25,733 --> 00:01:31,267
The youth hostel begins in 1912, with a teacher in Germany.

19
00:01:31,367 --> 00:01:37,300
A teacher named Richard Schirrmann was taking his students on hiking trips

20
00:01:37,400 --> 00:01:42,600
when he realized there was nowhere cheap and safe for children to stay.

21
00:01:42,700 --> 00:01:49,967
So he borrowed a school building and made lodging just for young people. That became today's youth hostel.

22
00:01:50,667 --> 00:01:52,467
So, to sum up:

23
00:01:52,567 --> 00:01:54,300
Hotels, for nobles.

24
00:01:54,400 --> 00:01:56,700
Motels, for cars.

25
00:01:56,800 --> 00:01:59,067
Youth hostels, for children.

26
00:02:00,767 --> 00:02:05,133
The next day, I explained it to my kid again.

27
00:02:05,233 --> 00:02:08,400
"Dad didn't really answer you yesterday, did he?"

28
00:02:08,500 --> 00:02:13,500
"The truth is, these three were made for different people."

29
00:02:13,600 --> 00:02:18,700
"Hotels started in big houses where important guests used to stay,"

30
00:02:18,800 --> 00:02:25,633
"motels were made so people driving far could sleep right next to their cars,"

31
00:02:25,733 --> 00:02:30,933
"and youth hostels were made by a teacher, for kids like you who go traveling."

32
00:02:31,033 --> 00:02:33,000
Then my kid asked:

33
00:02:33,100 --> 00:02:35,733
"So we can go to a youth hostel too, right?"

34
00:02:35,833 --> 00:02:39,900
"Yep. It was made exactly for kids like you."

35
00:02:40,600 --> 00:02:45,067
Even a single name hides who it was made for.

36
00:02:45,767 --> 00:02:52,667
Next time: why apartments, villas and officetels ended up with different names.
